{
    "name": "Neck Extensor Stretch",
    "description": "This illustration depicts the 'Neck Extensor Stretch' exercise, which targets the neck extensor muscles. The figure is shown from the back, with hands interlocked behind the head, gently pulling the chin toward the chest. The muscles highlighted in red and labeled include the Semispinalis capitis, Splenius capitis, Scalene, and Upper trapezius. The accompanying text on page 2 identifies the Upper trapezius as the most-stretched muscle, with the Longissimus capitis, semispinalis capitis, splenius capitis, and scalene as lesser-stretched muscles. This stretch is part of a sequence designed to improve neck flexibility, as introduced in Chapter 1, which discusses the anatomy and common causes of neck stiffness."
}

{
    "name": "Neck Extensor Stretch",
    "description": "This is a sketch of a person performing a neck stretch, with one hand placed on the back of the head and the head tilted slightly forward. The image is associated with the 'Neck Extensor Stretch' exercise, which is detailed in the text on the following page. The text identifies the muscles stretched, including the upper trapezius, and provides a technique for performing the stretch while sitting or standing upright."
}

{
    "name": "Neck Flexor Stretch",
    "description": "This image illustrates the Neck Flexor Stretch. The technique involves sitting or standing upright, interlocking hands and placing the palms on the forehead, then pulling the head back so the nose points straight up. The muscles stretched are the sternocleidomastoid (most-stretched), with lesser-stretched muscles including the longissimus capitis, semispinalis capitis, and splenius capitis. The image highlights the sternocleidomastoid, splenius capitis, semispinalis capitis, and scalene muscles."
}

{
    "name": "Neck Flexor Stretch",
    "description": "This is a diagram illustrating the Neck Flexor Stretch exercise. The image shows a person in a standing position with their head tilted back and hands interlocked on the forehead. The stretch targets the neck flexor muscles, with the sternocleidomastoid being the most stretched muscle, and the longissimus capitis, semispinalis capitis, and scalene being the lesser-stretched muscles. The technique involves sitting or standing upright, interlocking hands on the forehead, and pulling the head back so the nose points straight up."
}

{
    "name": "Neck Flexor and Rotation Stretch",
    "description": "This image illustrates a neck stretch targeting the flexor muscles. The accompanying text on page 5 (image 2) describes the 'Neck Flexor and Rotation Stretch' technique, which involves pulling the head back and toward the right to stretch the left sternocleidomastoid, longissimus capitis, semispinalis capitis, and splenius capitis. The image on page 6 (image 3) further details neck muscle movements, showing which muscles are stretched during flexion, extension, rotation, lateral flexion, and lateral extension, providing context for the targeted muscles in the stretch."
}

